<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Siege</p><h1 class="enc-title">Siege of Kerman</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1794">1794</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as final siege at Kerman</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a> was the final siege in 1794 in which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> leader Agha Mohammad Khan captured the city of Kerman from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lotf-ali-khan">Lotf Ali Khan</a>, the last Zand ruler. The city's inhabitants were punished by mass blinding. Lotf Ali Khan was captured and tortured to death, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zand-dynasty">Zand dynasty</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/siege-of-kerman.jpg" alt="Siege of Kerman" width="264" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Capture_and_Sack_of_Kerman_by_Agha_Mohammad_Khan_Qajar.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Afghan chiefs of Bam invited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lotf-ali-khan">Lotf Ali Khan</a>, the last Khan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zand-dynasty">Zand dynasty</a>, to return and throw off the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> yoke. With their help he came back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a> and captured the city on 30 March. His defiance deepened the hatred of his arch-rival, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agha-mohammad-khan-qajar">Agha Mohammad Khan Qajar</a>: every evening the people of Kerman recited poems from the towers by the gate mocking the Qajar leader, and Lotf Ali Khan struck a coin in his own name.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Agha Mohammad Khan heard of the capture quickly and marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a> on 14 May. The siege took a heavy toll on the city's population. Kerman fell on 24 October, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lotf-ali-khan">Lotf Ali Khan</a> fled to Bam, but the chief of Bam handed him over to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajars</a>. Delivered to the rival who had long waited for revenge, he is reported to have been blinded; he was imprisoned and tortured in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> and choked to death late in 1794.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Agha Mohammad Khan took a brutal revenge on the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a> for harbouring his enemy. All the male inhabitants were killed or blinded, and a pile of 20,000 eyeballs was poured out before the victorious <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> leader. The women and children were sold into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, and the city was destroyed over ninety days. Tens of thousands of its people were killed, blinded or enslaved in all, and it took Kerman decades to recover. With the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lotf-ali-khan">Lotf Ali Khan</a> the last of the Zand rulers was gone.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Siege of Kerman” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1794, after the long <b>Siege of Kerman</b>, Agha Mohammad Khan took the city, and Lotf Ali Khan, betrayed as he fled at Bam, was captured, tortured and put to death.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zand-empire">Zand Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
